  "source_claims": [
    {
      "claim": "A numeric *0x address is a nonempty ADDRESS_LOCATION_SPEC. Its decoder passes the numeric address to find_pc_line; find_pc_sect_line binds SAL.pspace to current_program_space. Passing search_pspace does not select that owner.",
      "refs": [
        "gdb/location.c:122-141",
        "gdb/location.c:789-798",
        "gdb/linespec.c:3053-3070",
        "gdb/linespec.c:2169-2181",
        "gdb/symtab.c:3224-3234"
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The inferior setter clears/redecodes locations using a target pspace filter without selecting that program space. The actual location inherits SAL.pspace. Inferior readback alone does not prove actual numeric location ownership.",
      "refs": [
        "gdb/python/py-breakpoint.c:307-368",
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:1632-1682",
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:13187-13327",
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:8626-8638"
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Ordinary fork creates a distinct program space. A properly established parent-restricted breakpoint does not simply fan out into every child through reset; the supported counterexample is construction of the deferred final-child site under restored intermediate selection.",
      "refs": [
        "gdb/infrun.c:626-633",
        "gdb/progspace.c:232-249",
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:13187-13327"
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Actual insertion uses location program space. A missed child one-shot can leave an old numeric location eligible for later reset; this is a static possible route, without attribution of the historical error.",
      "refs": [
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:3384-3401",
        "gdb/breakpoint.h:839-854",
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:4007-4024",
        "gdb/breakpoint.c:11993-12003",
        "gdb/infrun.c:1387-1394"
      ]
    }
  ],
